From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 07:39:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: a733-cubie-a7s: read the MAC address
 from the EEPROM

The Cubie A7S carries a factory EEPROM that holds the MAC address of the
board. The kernel cannot read it, so the MAC address depends on what the
boot firmware puts into the device tree.

Enable TWI0 on PG8 and PG9 and describe U5 (BL24C16F, 16 kbit) at 0x50;
the chip answers on 0x50 to 0x57 (eight 256-byte blocks). VCC is ALDO1.
The write protect pin is held high by R58 and pulled low through Q2 while
PC7 is high (schematic V1.10 p.16). An asserted wp-gpios means protected,
so PC7 is GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW. The node is read-only: the contents are
factory data and the driver must not write them.

A fixed-layout nvmem layout names the cell of the six-byte MAC address at
offset 0x9e, and the GMAC0 node takes it as "mac-address". The
fixed-layout driver is built with NVMEM_LAYOUTS, which the arm64 defconfig
has. of_get_mac_address() tries the "mac-address", "local-mac-address" and
"address" properties first and the nvmem cell last, so a
local-mac-address from the boot firmware still takes precedence. The
EEPROM driver and the TWI driver are already enabled by the defconfig
(EEPROM_AT24 as a module, I2C_MV64XXX).

Not covered: writing the EEPROM, and the other fields of its contents.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: with no address from the boot loader, GMAC0
takes the address in the EEPROM; without the cell it takes a random one.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts   | 32 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
index d7acd2fee254..2339be766504 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -247,6 +247,8 @@ wifi@1 {
 &gmac0 {
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&rgmii0_ph_pins>;
+	nvmem-cells = <&eeprom_mac>;
+	nvmem-cell-names = "mac-address";
 	phy-handle = <&ext_rgmii_phy>;
 	/*
 	 * No delay lines on the board. The PHY adds no delay: it has no
@@ -285,6 +287,36 @@ &gpu {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+&i2c0 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c0_pg_pins>;
+	status = "okay";
+
+	/*
+	 * U5, 16 kbit, eight 256-byte blocks at 0x50..0x57. Holds the
+	 * factory data of the board. Read only: the driver does not write
+	 * it. WP is pulled high (protected) by R58; Q2 pulls it low while
+	 * PC7 is high.
+	 */
+	eeprom@50 {
+		compatible = "belling,bl24c16f", "atmel,24c16";
+		reg = <0x50>;
+		vcc-supply = <&reg_aldo1>;
+		wp-gpios = <&pio 2 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>; /* PC7 */
+		read-only;
+
+		nvmem-layout {
+			compatible = "fixed-layout";
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <1>;
+
+			eeprom_mac: mac-address@9e {
+				reg = <0x9e 6>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
+
 &mixer0 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
